# Target C-II — exceptional-face train boundary versus internal flex dimension

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only exact network theorem
**Scope:** canonical connected positive-contact core with the audited stopped-train decomposition
**Dependencies:** finite train primitives; audited exceptional-face closure matrix
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_exceptional_host_escape_or_flex.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

There is an exact expansion/flex alternative for every collection of bounded nonquadrilateral stopping faces. It packages the high-`t` pentagon sea into a host-network statement without using any scalar endpoint or phase theorem.

Let `\mathcal S` be any set of bounded nonquadrilateral faces. Write

\[
F_{\mathcal S}:=|\mathcal S|,
\qquad
Q_{\mathcal S}:=\sum_{F\in\mathcal S}(\deg F-4).
\]

Let

- `E_int(\mathcal S)` be the number of complete stopped trains whose two terminal faces both lie in `\mathcal S`;
- `E_\partial(\mathcal S)` be the number of complete stopped trains with exactly one terminal face in `\mathcal S`.

The audited no-bounded-return theorem excludes a nontrivial train terminating twice on one bounded face, and bounded singleton loops are excluded by the endpoint geometry. Therefore every boundary occurrence of every face in `\mathcal S` is counted once by an escaping train and twice by an internal train. Hence

\[
\boxed{
4F_{\mathcal S}+Q_{\mathcal S}
=2E_{int}(\mathcal S)+E_\partial(\mathcal S).
}
\tag{HF.1}
\]

## 1. Internal train columns give a supported closure kernel

Restrict the canonical face-closure matrix to

- the `2F_{\mathcal S}` scalar rows belonging to faces of `\mathcal S`; and
- the columns of trains internal to `\mathcal S`.

Call this matrix `C_{\mathcal S,int}`. An internal train has no terminal occurrence on any bounded exceptional face outside `\mathcal S`, so its full `C_face` column is zero outside the selected rows. Thus every vector in `ker C_{\mathcal S,int}` extends by zero to a vector in the global kernel `ker C_face`.

Since the selected matrix has only `2F_{\mathcal S}` rows,

\[
\dim\ker C_{\mathcal S,int}
\ge E_{int}(\mathcal S)-2F_{\mathcal S}.
\]

Substituting (HF.1) gives the exact excess formula

\[
\boxed{
\dim\ker C_{\mathcal S,int}
\ge {Q_{\mathcal S}-E_\partial(\mathcal S)\over2}.
}
\tag{HF.2}
\]

When the right side is negative this is only the trivial nonnegative-nullity statement. When positive it is a genuine finite lower bound.

The train-rate vector of a global infinitesimal rotation spans at most one dimension of this supported kernel. Using the audited identification of `ker C_face` with contact infinitesimal flexes modulo translations, the non-Euclidean contact-flex dimension generated by internal trains obeys

\[
\boxed{
\dim\mathcal V_{\mathcal S}
\ge {Q_{\mathcal S}-E_\partial(\mathcal S)\over2}-1.
}
\tag{HF.3}
\]

The localization is in train-rate coordinates; the integrated vertex velocity field need not have compact vertex support.

## 2. Pareto form

For every fixed `0<=eta<=1`,

\[
E_\partial(\mathcal S)<\eta Q_{\mathcal S}
\Longrightarrow
\dim\mathcal V_{\mathcal S}>{1-\eta\over2}Q_{\mathcal S}-1.
\tag{HF.4}
\]

In particular,

\[
\boxed{
E_\partial(\mathcal S)\ge {Q_{\mathcal S}\over2}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\dim\mathcal V_{\mathcal S}\ge {Q_{\mathcal S}\over4}-1.
}
\tag{HF.5}
\]

This is a Cheeger-type statement for the stopped-train host network: exceptional-degree excess must either leave the selected host set or appear as internal contact-flex dimension.

## 3. Three useful specializations

### 3.1 All pentagons

For the set of all pentagonal faces, every face has degree five, so

\[
Q_{\mathcal S}=P_5.
\]

Thus (HF.5) is exactly the pentagon-sea escape-or-flex theorem.

### 3.2 Fully rhombic pentagons

For any selected set of fully rhombic pentagons, again `Q_{\mathcal S}=|\mathcal S|`. This recovers the flower escape-or-flex theorem, now as a special case of one finite identity.

### 3.3 All bounded exceptional faces

Take `\mathcal S` to be every bounded nonquadrilateral face. Then

\[
Q_{\mathcal S}=Q,
\]

and a train counted by `E_\partial(\mathcal S)` cannot terminate at another bounded exceptional host outside the set. Its other terminal occurrence is on the actual outer face. Hence, if `E_{BO}` denotes bounded-to-outer complete stopped trains,

\[
\boxed{
E_{BO}\ge {Q\over2}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\dim\mathcal V_{BB}\ge {Q\over4}-1.
}
\tag{HF.6}
\]

This strictly sharpens the escape conclusion of the pentagon-only form.

## 4. High-`t` corollary through `t<=2.02`

Every pentagon contributes one unit to `Q`, so `Q>=P_5`. The uniform pentagon sea gives

\[
{P_5\over D}\ge p_5(T)-o(1),
\qquad
p_5(T)=\pi+C_{707}-{5T\over2}.
\]

Equation (HF.6) therefore gives the exact outward-certified asymptotic alternatives

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
T & Q/D\ \text{floor} & E_{BO}/D & \dim\mathcal V_{BB}/D\\ \hline
1.880 & >0.3539 & >0.1769\ \text{or} & >0.0884\\
1.900 & >0.3039 & >0.1519\ \text{or} & >0.0759\\
1.920 & >0.2539 & >0.1269\ \text{or} & >0.0634\\
1.960 & >0.1539 & >0.0769\ \text{or} & >0.0384\\
2.000 & >0.0539 & >0.0269\ \text{or} & >0.0134\\
2.010 & >0.0289 & >0.0144\ \text{or} & >0.0072\\
2.020 & >0.0039 & >0.0019\ \text{or} & >0.0009
\end{array}
\tag{HF.7}
\]

The `-1` in (HF.6) is negligible after normalization by `D`.

## 5. Remaining obstruction

Canonical mixed infinitesimal rigidity makes the actual-diameter derivative map injective on the non-Euclidean space `\mathcal V_{BB}`. Thus the flex branch demands a linear-dimensional diameter-blocking image.

The escape branch now consists of actual bounded-to-outer trains. These are not outer-to-outer trains, so the audited one-train Jordan shear and two-sided diameter-bracing theorem do not apply directly. The shortest next theorem is therefore either

1. a one-bounded/one-outer train bracing or finite-motion theorem; or
2. a host-network argument converting many bounded-to-outer trains into a forbidden closure-rank/diameter-rank pattern.

**Disposition:** proved exact exceptional-host boundary-versus-flex theorem and its high-`t` bounded-to-outer corollary. C-II and Level I remain open.
